<section id="start">
  <p class="eyebrow">Schemas / Migration</p>

  <h1>Migrating from v0.3 to v0.4</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    You only need to migrate a document if you want its v0.4 lifecycle fields.
    Documents stay valid against the hosted v0.3 contracts until you move them.
    For each document you do move, the work is small: point
    <code>$schema</code> at the v0.4 URL, set <code>schemaVersion</code> to
    <code>"0.4.0"</code>, and rewrite any lifecycle metadata in the new shape.
  </p>

  <h2>Start here</h2>

  <p>
    v0.4 is a lifecycle release. It standardizes a canonical
    <code>status</code> field across every manifest entity, centralizes
    deprecation records in a new shared
    <a href="/docs/schemas/v0.4/lifecycle/">lifecycle fragment</a>, accepts an
    explicit <code>deprecated: false</code> assertion in native manifests, and
    moves every contract URL from the v0.3 <code>/schemas/v0.3/…</code> addresses to
    <code>/schemas/v0.4/…</code>. Nothing is converted automatically, and
    migrating is an explicit, document-by-document or profile-by-profile
    decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide compares v0.4 with the hosted v0.3 contracts: the snapshot
    served from <code>/schemas/v0.3/</code>, whose <code>$id</code> values are
    <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/v0.3/&lt;file&gt;</code>.
    The v0.3 copy shipped inside <code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code> is
    already stricter: its platform mappings are closed, <code>usage</code> is
    removed, and its <code>$id</code> differs. Validating against that copy
    surfaces the platform-mapping break before you migrate. Package 0.4.0
    tightened the v0.3 contracts as well, so you meet that change when you
    upgrade the package, whether or not you move documents to v0.4.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="at-a-glance">
  <h2>At a glance</h2>

  <h3>Required, and may break existing documents</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Native manifests: new <code>$schema</code> URL under
      <code>/schemas/v0.4/</code>, and <code>schemaVersion: "0.4.0"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>message</code> in a deprecation record must be a non-empty string.
    </li>
    <li>
      Platform mappings in tokens and DTCG extensions are closed, and
      <code>usage</code> is removed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Optional</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A <code>status</code> field on every entity.</li>
    <li>An explicit <code>deprecated: false</code> in native manifests.</li>
    <li>Per-entity lifecycle extensions in CEM manifests.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="checklist">
  <h2>Migration checklist</h2>

  <h3>All documents</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Inventory.</strong> List your v0.3 documents (tokens, utilities,
      icons manifests; CEM manifests; DTCG files with
      <code>recipes.designlasagna</code> extensions) and the consumers of each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose what moves.</strong> Migrate only documents whose consumers
      support v0.4. Everything else stays on v0.3, which remains published.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Native track</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Declare the format.</strong> Set <code>$schema</code> to the v0.4
      URL (for example
      <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json</code>) and
      <code>schemaVersion: "0.4.0"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rewrite lifecycle metadata.</strong> Add <code>status</code> where
      relevant and convert <code>deprecated</code> to the record shape. Use
      <code>deprecated: false</code> only where an explicit negative assertion
      is wanted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Drop removed data.</strong> Remove <code>PlatformMapping.usage</code>
      and any custom platform keys from tokens.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>CEM and DTCG track</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>CEM.</strong> Keep the standard CEM <code>schemaVersion</code>;
      author <code>status</code> and <code>deprecated</code> in the extension
      fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>DTCG.</strong> Keep the standard <code>$deprecated</code>
      boolean/string mirror; put the structured record in
      <code>recipes.designlasagna</code>; add <code>status</code> where useful;
      remove <code>PlatformMapping.usage</code> and any custom platform keys.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Select the profile.</strong> Select lifecycle profile
      <code>0.4</code> in the consuming project or resolver config.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Both tracks</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Tooling.</strong> Install <code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code>
      (or a later 0.4.x), import from the <code>v0.4/</code> paths, and register
      all six v0.4 schemas by <code>$id</code> for offline validation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Validate both directions.</strong> Migrated documents validate
      against the v0.4 contracts; unmigrated documents continue to validate
      against v0.3.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<aside class="schema-install" aria-labelledby="install-contracts">
  <div class="schema-install__intro">
    <h2 id="install-contracts">Install the contracts</h2>
    <p>
      Both formats ship in the same npm package. Pin the package release
      (<code>0.4.0</code>) for installation; use the format version
      (<code>v0.4</code>) in schema URLs and import paths.
    </p>
  </div>
  <dl class="schema-install__sources">
    <div>
      <dt>npm</dt>
      <dd><code>npm install @designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>v0.4 import (migrated documents)</dt>
      <dd><code>import schema from '@designlasagna/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json' with { type: 'json' };</code></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>v0.3 import (unmigrated documents)</dt>
      <dd><code>import schema from '@designlasagna/schemas/v0.3/tokens.json' with { type: 'json' };</code></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</aside>

